v32opt -- current known issues and limitations
(only what is still open; fixed problems are in the git history)

Legend: [limit] = a deliberate limit of the analysis, the optimizer stays
correct but leaves optimization on the table; [gap] = missing optimization or
tooling; [bug] = wrong or unpleasant behavior; [doc] = documentation.


PASSES

[limit] Hand-written assembly gains little from -O2/-O3.
  promote-*, omit-frame-pointers, and the loop passes recognize compiler
  output: a "PUSH BP / MOV BP,SP / ISUB SP,N" frame, "__function_*" labels,
  loops of the form "start: test / JF end / body / step / JMP start".
  Frameless, register-passing code has no frame to promote and is mostly
  left alone (primes.asm: -O3 gains 12% where compiled C gains 62%).

[limit] The loop passes (loop-invert, loop-unroll, loop-unroll-partial,
  loop-index, loop-strength) match the loop shape the C compilers emit.
  v32lua's loops have a different shape and are not touched: Lua gains from
  -O1/-O2 only.
  A "while"/"for" with several exits, a test longer than 8 instructions (a
  setting), labels that are referenced from outside the loop, or a CALL in the
  body (loop-unroll, loop-unroll-partial, loop-index, loop-strength) are
  skipped.

[limit] loop-index folds one constant base into the counter, needs an
  immediate bound, and gives up when the counter is used for anything else
  (a value, two bases, a CALL, live after the loop). Some of those loops are
  taken by loop-strength instead (next entry).

[limit] loop-strength (-O3) rewrites the address groups "base + K*i" that the
  compilers emit (unit, scaled by IMUL/SHL, frame LEA, "arr[i + c]") in
  counted loops into a stepped pointer register. It does not handle:
  - a base reloaded from memory in every iteration (a pointer parameter:
    "MOV Rt, [BP+2] / IADD Rt, Ri"); the base must be an immediate or a
    register nothing in the loop writes;
  - an address built from two counters (the inner loop of a two-dimensional
    array);
  - a loop with a CALL in the body (the pointer register would have to
    survive the callee);
  - the frame form ("LEA Rt, [BP+off]") unless the input has the "escapes="
    lines of v32cc --emit-hints; a scaled frame base at all;
  - removing the counter when the pointer makes it redundant (that needs an
    end pointer for a runtime bound).
  It makes at most two pointers per loop, and only when each saves at least
  one instruction per iteration net of its step. Spec:
  doc/pointer-strength-reduction.md.

[limit] promote-regs, promote-leaf and promote-loops add no fresh register
  save (PUSH/POP) to a function in which an "[SP+k]" operand can reach the
  saved word: a single "ISUB SP,N" that reserves locals and outgoing
  arguments together, with the arguments stored by "MOV [SP+k], x", would let
  the save overwrite an argument. That is every v32cc function that passes
  stack arguments when the input has no ";@hints" header and so is read as
  callee-save (with "abi=caller-save" nothing needs saving), and hand-written
  code of the same layout. The stock compiler's separate "ISUB SP,argwords"
  is not affected. Such a function can only promote into registers it already
  saves.

[limit] In caller-save mode (--abi caller-save, or a ";@hints" header with
  abi=caller-save) the promote passes use registers without saving them. liveness.c only
  treats as outputs of a function the registers it wrote when the session
  began, so a register written by a promote pass is not live at the RET: a
  caller that keeps a value in that register across the call, against the
  convention, is not protected. Compiler output (v32cc, v32lua) never does;
  hand-written assembly declared caller-save must follow the convention.

[limit] loop-unroll-partial: a runtime bound within (K-1)*|step| of the 32-bit
  limit would wrap in the guard. No real bound is; there is no check.

[limit] peephole-jumps (unreachable-code elimination) does not reason about
  computed jump targets: after "JMP R0" / "JMP 0x1000" the following code is
  left alone.

[limit] liveness.c (peephole-zero-test, peephole-dead-regs, promote-*, the loop
  passes) answers for R0..R10 only. Functions are grouped into classes: code
  that control can enter from another function without a CALL (a JMP/JT/JF
  into it, or fall-through into its label) belongs to the same class as the
  function that jumps or falls into it, and a RET is charged to the callers of
  its whole class. A class that is only reached indirectly (no direct CALL),
  or whose address is taken (its label is an operand other than a CALL/JMP
  target, or in a data directive), has all registers live at its RET. The
  callee summaries are computed once per session and are not refreshed when a
  pass removes instructions: they stay sound only because the passes remove
  nothing but writes proven dead (stale answers are more conservative, never
  less). Two limits remain: a save slot overwritten or read through [SP+k] or
  [BP-k] aliasing is not detected, and a CALL/JMP to a numeric address is
  "unknown" at the call site (all live) but cannot make a function "address
  taken".

[limit] Disassembled CARTs: ROM data is referenced by absolute address, which
  v32opt does not relocate, so any size-changing pass breaks them.

[gap] After full unrolling a loop, omit-frame-pointers removes the orphaned
  "PUSH Rn / POP Rn" and an unused "ISUB SP,N", and peephole-repeat-imm and
  peephole-copies the repeated "MOV Rd, imm". What is left: an "ISUB SP,N"
  is removed whole or not at all (a frame that could shrink is not shrunk);
  the unreferenced labels of the copies stay in the output text (they cost
  no word in the assembled program); a memory reload repeated in every copy
  ("MOV R0, [BP+2]" in each copy of a body that stores a parameter) is not
  removed.

[gap] constant-folding trades register operands for immediates ("MOV Rd, Rs"
  -> "MOV Rd, const" is 2 words instead of 1), so the assembled program can
  grow; its payoff is enabling the immediate-based peepholes. A post-fold
  "re-materialize into a register" pass would recover the size.

[gap] Profile-guided decisions cover the loop passes only. Function inlining
  and the promote passes do not look at the profile.

[bug] peephole-zero-test: the rewritten jump loses its trailing source comment.

[bug] A ";@v" hint on an instruction without a memory operand ("MOV R1, 5
  ;@v") makes v32opt print "a volatile access was optimized away or
  rewritten ... please report this" at every optimization level including
  -O0, although nothing was changed: hints_verify() (src/hints.c) requires
  every marked instruction to have an indirect operand. doc/hints.md says to
  mark only the load or store, so the input is wrong, but the message blames
  v32opt.

[bug] omit-frame-pointers (frame cleanup): function A jumps into the
  epilogue of function B (a shared tail: "jt R0, __b_tail"), both with
  "PUSH R4 ... POP R4", A uses R4 and B does not. B's save looks orphaned
  and is removed with its POP, so A's jump no longer restores R4. Compiler
  output has no such jump; hand-written code and genasm can.
  Reproducer: testing/frame-cleanup/shared_tail.asm (not in run.sh).

[bug] peephole-movs deletes a repeated "MOV R12, imm" (R11/R13 too) after a
  MOVS, SETS or CMPS, which write R11-R13 without naming them
  (modifies_register() does not know). Present before the 20261109 changes.
  Reproducer: testing/peephole-repeat-imm/known_peephole_movs.asm
  (not in run.sh). peephole-repeat-imm itself handles them.

[bug] dce drops a "__function_*" label that is only fallen into from the
  function above it (the preceding function then runs on into the next one),
  and a function that is only entered by a jump to a label inside it (the jump
  target becomes undefined). Present before the 20261109 changes.
  "__function_a: mov R0,1 / __function_b: iadd R0,2 / ret" with
  "call __function_a" elsewhere loses __function_b at -fdce.

[bug] The stock Vircon32 compiler is not consistently callee-save (a function
  only ever called at statement level may write R1 without saving it).
  v32opt never adds such a write itself (testing/lint checks that against
  the input), but cannot repair the compiler's own.


TOOLING AND TESTS

[limit] --trigger-max counts one slot per deleted node in most passes, one per
  4-node group, per removed register save and per removed ISUB in
  omit-frame-pointers, one per swept function in dce, one per promoted slot
  in promote-*, one per rewritten loop in the loop passes (loop-index and
  loop-strength included), one per deleted MOV in peephole-repeat-imm.
  Bisection is consistent within a pass, not across passes.

[limit] scripts/v32prof.py profiles what the program does on its own. A game
  that needs input to reach its hot paths is profiled in its attract mode
  unless the workload is built into the program, or run for more frames.
  It needs a v32sim with --profile-out (the dev branch).

[limit] bench.py --tune is a greedy search (one change at a time, at most 8),
  not exhaustive; every candidate is a full build and run, so large programs
  (crisp: 700K lines of assembly) take minutes per candidate.

[fixed] Operand names were kept in a 32-byte field, so two symbols sharing their first
  31 characters compared equal and the dead-store pass deleted a live store
  (crisp: global_ringblastEnemyStoneAngle / ...AngleVel). Now 128 bytes;
  testing/long-names.

[limit] crisp-game-lib is timing-sensitive (see testing/crisp/README.md): builds of
  different speed can diverge in the last games even with a fixed seed.

[limit] Whole-RAM comparison of real programs works only when they are
  deterministic: use v32sim --fixed-clock/--seed/--fixed-cycles. A program
  that seeds its RNG from the cycle counter (crisp-game-lib) differs between
  builds of different speed unless --fixed-cycles is used.

[limit] testing/differential and testing/bench need the sibling projects built
  (v32as, v32sim; v32cc, the stock "compile", v32c++, v32lua for their
  languages) and are not part of "make tests". Seven suites of "make tests"
  (peephole-dead-regs, loop-strength, frame-cleanup, peephole-repeat-imm,
  peephole-copies-imm, constant-folding-entries, promote-save) run programs
  of testing/differential through its run.py and need v32as and v32sim, but
  no compiler. "make tests" itself needs the Vircon32 DevTools for its unit
  fixtures (testing/primes also needs v32lua).

[bug] A few small memory leaks (nodes made by passes) are reported by
  LeakSanitizer at exit; no memory errors under AddressSanitizer/UBSan.

[doc] USAGE.md is English only (the README and the manual pages exist in
  English, Spanish and French).
